 :findings
 [{:id :s4a-dispatch-code-01 :severity :P2
   :title "Persistent collection backing-store accessors execute before refusal"
   :locations ["src/oikos/extern/dispatch_codec.cljs:17-43" "src/oikos/law/dispatch.cljc:30-56" "src/oikos/shape/dispatch.cljc:9-15"]
   :proof "In the pinned NBB runtime, construct x=(f/envelope (first f/requests)), a=(aget x \"C\"), v=(aget a 1), and hits=(atom 0). Define property 1 of a using Object.defineProperty with a getter that increments hits and returns v. (law/bounded-data? x) returned true with hits=1. After reset, (codec/encode x) returned :encoded with hits=5. Thus own-descriptor checks on the outer persistent map do not inspect the backing JS array before Clojure iteration invokes its accessor. Similarly for v=[123 125], a=(aget v \"Kb\"); define getter 0 incrementing hits and returning 123; (shape/frame v) returned :framed with hits=2. The backing property names are observed on the pinned NBB, not a portable API. No transport was invoked in these probes: getters only incremented an in-memory atom."
   :impact "A forged persistent collection can run caller code inside supposedly inert validation/encoding/framing, including any effect available to that caller, while being accepted. This contradicts the no-caller-accessor and plain-data/no-effects boundaries; constant refusal catches exceptions only after the accessor has already run. It is not a claim of runtime authorization or an actual network effect."
   :required-regression "Before traversal or coercion, refuse forged backing arrays, accessor/proxy-backed nested storage, method/prototype tampering and metadata/records for all relevant public plain-data/framing/encoding inputs without invoking the getter/proxy. Include root and nested map/vector cases and verify zero counter plus constant refusal; preserve valid immutable values and both runtime suites. Avoid changing frozen files or broadening authorized scope."}]
